\section{Setting and method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Setting.}
A benchmark has $N$ items.
A \emph{target} model has an unknown binary vector $y\in\{0,1\}^N$ ($y_j=1$ if it answers item $j$ correctly) and a full-benchmark score $\mu=\frac1N\sum_j y_j$ that we want to know without running all items.
We hold the results $Y\in\{0,1\}^{M\times N}$ of $M$ \emph{source} models on every item.
We choose a sample $S$ of $n\ll N$ items using only $Y$, run the target on $S$ only, and output $\hat\mu$.
Quality is the error of $\hat\mu$ against $\mu$ (RMSE, signed bias) and how well the $\hat\mu$ of several targets orders them.
This is the estimation of a finite-population mean from $n$ of $N$ values, with a covariate known for all $N$ units, the columns of $Y$: the source models can reduce the \emph{variance} of $\hat\mu$, through the choice of $S$ and through the estimator, while the \emph{bias} is controlled by how $S$ is drawn.

\paragraph{Target splits.}
A predictor fitted on the sources is only supported where the targets resemble them, so we use three protocols that differ only in how models are divided into sources and targets.
\textbf{Interpolation}: five random folds of the models; the targets are one fold and the sources the other four.
\textbf{Lineage-held-out}: the same, over model families rather than models, so that no target has a family member among the sources.
\textbf{Extrapolation}: the targets are the top 25\% of models by full score and the sources are the rest, so that every target scores above every source.
Families are derived from model names by keyword (Appendix~\ref{app:families}).

\paragraph{Designs.}
A design draws $S$ of fixed size $n$ without replacement, with first-order inclusion probabilities $\pi_j$.
\emph{Uniform} sampling has $\pi_j=n/N$; \emph{stratified} sampling orders items by source difficulty and draws one item at random from each of $n$ consecutive blocks of $N/n$ items.
The \emph{PPS} design takes
\[
\pi_j \;=\; n\Big[(1-\varepsilon)\,\frac{w_j}{\sum_k w_k}+\frac{\varepsilon}{N}\Big],\qquad w_j=\sqrt{\hat p_j(1-\hat p_j)},
\]
where $\hat p_j$ is the fraction of sources answering item $j$ correctly (capped so that $\pi_j\le1$), drawn by systematic sampling in difficulty order.
It favours items on which the sources disagree, and the floor $\varepsilon n/N>0$ keeps every item drawable, which makes a design-based estimator unbiased whatever the target is.
The \emph{balanced} design selects greedily, from a random pool of $4n$ items, the $n$ whose mean reproduces the full scores of all sources; it is a greedy stand-in for the cube method~\citep{deville2004cube}, without its exact inclusion probabilities.
\emph{Anchor} selection clusters the items by their source response vectors with $k$-means ($k=n$) and keeps the medoid of each cluster, weighted by cluster size~\citep{vivek2024anchor}; the clustering is Lloyd's algorithm~\citep{lloyd1982least}.
\emph{Information-top} selects, once for all targets, the $n$ items of highest Fisher information under the two-dimensional latent-factor model below, fitted on the sources (information of item $j$: $\|a_j\|^2$ times the mean over source abilities of $p(1-p)$); \emph{CAT} is adaptive: with a one-dimensional two-parameter logistic model it asks each target, in turn, the unseen item of largest information at its current ability estimate.

\paragraph{Predictors and estimators.}
A predictor $\hat p_j$ gives the probability that the target answers item $j$ correctly, given its answers $y_S$ and the sources.
We use three: a two-dimensional logistic latent-factor model, $\operatorname{logit}\hat p_j=\theta^\top a_j+c_j$, whose item parameters are fitted on the sources by penalised likelihood (a multidimensional extension of the two-parameter logistic model~\citep{birnbaum1968some,lord1980applications,baker2004irt}) and whose target ability $\theta$ is the posterior mode given the target's $n$ answers (\emph{IRT}; the item parameters are not refitted on the target); the $k$ sources nearest to the target by agreement on the sample (\emph{kNN}); and kernel ridge regression from sample responses to the full response vector (\emph{ridge}).
\emph{Calreg} is an estimator in itself: least squares across the sources of the full score on the Hájek sample score, evaluated at the target's sample score and clipped to $[0,1]$ (shared-subset designs only).
Four estimators combine a predictor with the sample.
The \emph{mean} estimator is the Hájek mean~\citep{hajek1971comment} $\sum_{j\in S}y_j/\pi_j\big/\sum_{j\in S}1/\pi_j$, a ratio form of the Horvitz--Thompson estimator~\citep{horvitz1952generalization}, and ignores the predictor.
The \emph{model} estimator is $\frac1N\sum_{j=1}^N\hat p_j$.
The \emph{plug-in} estimator is $\frac nN\bar y_H+\big(1-\frac nN\big)\bar p_{\bar S}$, the Hájek mean $\bar y_H$ weighted by the sampled share plus the mean prediction $\bar p_{\bar S}$ over the unsampled items.
The \emph{difference} estimator corrects the model estimator by the residuals on the sample,
\[
\hat\mu_\lambda \;=\; \frac1N\sum_{j=1}^N \hat p_j \;+\; \lambda\,\frac{\sum_{j\in S}(y_j-\hat p_j)/\pi_j}{\sum_{j\in S}1/\pi_j},\qquad \lambda\in[0,1],
\]
where for $j\in S$ the prediction $\hat p_j$ is cross-fitted~\citep{chernozhukov2018double} (the target-specific part of the predictor is refitted on four fifths of the sample and evaluated on the fifth left out) so that it does not see its own label.
For a fixed predictor and $\lambda=1$ this is the generalized-regression estimator of model-assisted survey sampling~\citep{sarndal1992model}, and with a weight $\lambda$ it is the power-tuned estimator of prediction-powered inference~\citep{angelopoulos2023ppiplus}: it is design-unbiased when the predictor and $\lambda$ are fixed beforehand, and $\lambda=0$ is the model estimator.
Since $\hat p$ is fitted on the target's own sample and $\lambda$ and $\varepsilon$ are tuned, these guarantees hold only approximately; we measure the resulting bias (Section~\ref{sec:results}).
Hyperparameters ($\varepsilon$, $\lambda$, $k$, the ridge penalty) are chosen for each benchmark, outer fold, $n$ and design by the RMSE of the inner targets on an inner split of the sources that mimics the outer split (a random fifth of the sources for interpolation, a family fold of the sources for lineage, the top 25\% of the sources for extrapolation); the targets are never used for choosing them.

\paragraph{Ordering.}
Two models run on the same items are compared through the paired difference of their sample means, whose variance depends on how often they disagree.
Given the disagreement rate, a sample-only reference gives the probability that $n$ items order two models of true gap $\delta$ correctly from their own answers alone; a method that borrows from the sources can beat it, so it is a reference and not a bound.
